 ## Background
-Feature-Informed Diffusion Evolution (FIDE) was introduced by Andrei-Timotei Ardelean, Michael Fischer, Tim Weyrich, and Tomáš Iser [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of existing black-box optimization methods (like CMA-ES and Bayesian optimization) which rely on scalar fitness values, discarding spatial information present in rendered images [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the principles of diffusion models for generative modeling and CMA-ES for derivative-free optimization, combining them to solve inverse rendering problems without differentiability requirements [S1, unofficial].
+FIDE was introduced by Andrei-Timotei Ardelean, Michael Fischer, Tim Weyrich, and Tomáš Iser in 2026 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of CMA-ES [Hansen 2006] and diffusion models [Li and He 2025], addressing the limitations of black-box optimization methods that rely on scalar loss values. The method was validated across diverse inverse problems including camera pose estimation, robot arm estimation, path tracing, vector splines, and Voronoi shaders [S1, unofficial].
 
